pub const TASKS_LIST: &str = "List tasks with filters (project, status, priority, estimate, assignee, due date, tags, epic, scope, blocked_by, blocks, text query, title, title_exact, source_file), sorting and pagination. Use q to locate a task by id, title, description, the id of its epic or a partial tag (e.g. q='LOG-4', q='26', q='est:'). Returns: {items, total} - items is the list of matching tasks, total is the count of all matching tasks before limit/offset. limit defaults to 100 (max 1000); use offset and total to paginate. Use fields to limit returned columns; the field names it accepts are listed in the description of the fields parameter, unknown names are ignored, and id, project_id and number always stay in the result. By default each task carries short_description (first line, ~120 chars) instead of the full description; set include_description to get the full text, in which case short_description is omitted. include_project attaches project names, include_scope attaches scope names, and neither filters; project_archived filters by project archival; include_deleted also returns soft-deleted tasks (hidden by default). Task id is the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' identifier (e.g. 'LOG-82'); number is the per-project sequence number (e.g. 82), unique only inside its project. project_id accepts one id or a list of ids; exclude_project_id drops the given projects (use it for 'every project except X'). The epic filter accepts a full epic id (e.g. 'LOG-5') or a list of them; scope_id likewise takes one scope id or a list, and null matches tasks without that link. has_epic and has_scope filter by the presence of an epic or a scope. The estimate filter takes one size or a list of them ('S', 'M', 'L'); tasks without an estimate are not matched. The assignee filter takes one name or a list of them and matches case-insensitively; tasks without an assignee are not matched. The due_before and due_after filters take a date (YYYY-MM-DD) and select the tasks due before it or on and after it; tasks without a due date are not matched, so the two together give a due window. sort accepts updated_at, created_at, closed_at, priority, number and title. The blocked_by filter returns tasks whose blocked_by list contains the given task, the blocks filter returns tasks whose blocks list contains the given task. The source_file filter matches the source file path of a task partially and case-insensitively, so a fragment such as 'backlog' selects every task imported from that file. Every task carries blocked_by (tasks that block me) and blocks (tasks I block) as id lists.";

pub const TASKS_GET: &str = "Get a single task by task_id. The task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'); a bare number is rejected because it is unique only inside a project. Returns: the task itself - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts}. For a task with is_epic=true the result also carries children_counts: the number of its child tasks per status (backlog, open, in-progress, blocked, done, cancelled) plus their total.";

pub const TASKS_CREATE: &str = "Create a task. Requires project_id and title; description, status, priority, estimate, assignee, due_at, blocked_reason, epic, scope, tags are optional. description is Markdown and formatting is rendered: a short log or config in a fenced block is welcome, source code belongs in the repository. status defaults to 'backlog', where the task waits for a later session, even if the work looks small. Pass 'open' only when you start on it right away, in this same session; 'later' from the user always means backlog. estimate is the size of the task: 'S', 'M' or 'L'. assignee is the person responsible for the task, due_at is its due date (YYYY-MM-DD). blocked_reason is one line of free text explaining a block outside the tracker and is only allowed when status is 'blocked'. epic_id accepts a full epic id (e.g. 'LOG-5') of the same project. scope is optional: pass the scope name (e.g. 'site') or its id as scope_id; a task with neither sits outside the project's scopes, and scopes_create {project_id, name} adds a scope. Tags are optional too, but they come from a closed dictionary: an unknown tag is rejected with the list of existing ones, and tags_create {name} adds a genuinely new tag. source_file names the document or file the task came from; it is provenance only, it is never verified and several tasks may share it. Returns: the created task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts} with a warnings array.";

pub const TASKS_UPDATE: &str = "Update fields of an existing task by task_id. Pass the fields to change next to task_id (e.g. {task_id: 'LOG-87', status: 'in-progress'}); a nested patch object {task_id, patch: {status}} is accepted too. description is Markdown and formatting is rendered: a short log or config in a fenced block is welcome, source code belongs in the repository. The task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). epic_id accepts a full epic id (e.g. 'LOG-5') of the task's current project, scope takes the name of a scope of that project (case-insensitive; scope_id takes the id) and scope: null clears it, estimate takes the size of the task ('S', 'M' or 'L') or null to clear it, assignee takes the name of the responsible person or null to clear it, due_at takes the due date (YYYY-MM-DD) or null to clear it, and blocked_reason is one line of free text explaining a block outside the tracker, allowed only on a blocked task; leaving status 'blocked' clears it. The call must name at least one field with a value: no field, a field set to undefined, an unknown field or an epic id that does not exist is an error, the task is left untouched. Returns: the updated task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts} with a changes array and a warnings array, which is not empty when the task is blocked with neither a blocker task nor a blocked_reason, or when an epic is closed while some of its subtasks are still open. Closing the last open subtask of an epic closes the epic itself with reason 'fixed' and returns it in closed_epic; reopening a subtask of an epic that was closed automatically reopens the epic in open and returns it in reopened_epic. An unknown tag is rejected: tags are a closed dictionary, see tags_list and tags_create.";

pub const TASKS_CLOSE: &str = "Close a task by task_id with a reason: 'fixed' \u{2192} done, 'wontfix' | 'obsolete' | 'duplicate' | 'not-reproduced' \u{2192} cancelled. 'not-reproduced' marks a report that could not be reproduced. The task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). Closing a task that blocks others releases them from status 'blocked' - every released task returns to the status it had before it was blocked ('open' when that status is unknown), and those tasks are listed in released. The blocking links stay in place as history, so do not remove them afterwards. Returns: the closed task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts} with a changes array, a released array and a warnings array, which is not empty when an epic is closed while some of its subtasks are still open. Closing the last open subtask of an epic closes the epic itself with reason 'fixed' and returns it in closed_epic.";

pub const TASKS_CREATE_MANY: &str = "Create several tasks in one call. Each item is created independently and the response reports ok/error per item; a failing item does not cancel the others. Items are created in the given order, so task numbers are allocated sequentially. Each item needs its own project_id and title and accepts the same fields as tasks_create, including epic_id as a full epic id of the item's project. Returns: {items, succeeded, failed} - every item is {index, id, ok, task, changes, released, warnings} when it worked and {index, id, ok:false, error:{kind, message}} when it failed, where index is the position in the request list and id is the id of the created task (null for a failed item). changes is empty for a created task, released is empty when nothing was released. Every item carries its own warnings array. Tags are a closed dictionary here as well: an unknown tag fails that item and leaves the others untouched.";

pub const TASKS_UPDATE_MANY: &str = "Update several tasks in one call. Each item is applied independently and the response reports ok/error per item; a failing item does not cancel the others. Every item carries task_id plus the fields to change next to it (e.g. {items: [{task_id: 'LOG-87', status: 'done'}]}); a nested patch object {task_id, patch: {...}} is accepted too. Every task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form. Every item must name at least one field with a value: no field, a field set to undefined, an unknown field or an epic id that does not exist is an error for that item, the task is left untouched and the item reports ok:false. Returns: {items, succeeded, failed} - every item is {index, id, ok, task, changes, released, warnings} when it worked and {index, id, ok:false, error:{kind, message}} when it failed, where index is the position in the request list and id is the id of the task. released is empty when nothing was released. Every successful item carries its own warnings array. An unknown tag fails that item only: tags are a closed dictionary, see tags_list and tags_create.";

pub const TASKS_CLOSE_MANY: &str = "Close several tasks in one call. Each item is applied independently and the response reports ok/error per item; a failing item does not cancel the others. Every task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form. Returns: {items, succeeded, failed} - every item is {index, id, ok, task, changes, released, warnings} when it worked and {index, id, ok:false, error:{kind, message}} when it failed, where index is the position in the request list and id is the id of the task.";

pub const TASKS_DELETE: &str = "Soft-delete a task by task_id. The task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). Deleting drops the blocker links of the task and detaches it from its epic, so the affected tasks change as well - they are listed in released. Returns: {id, deleted_at, changes, released}.";

pub const TASKS_RESTORE: &str = "Restore a soft-deleted task by task_id: clears deleted_at and the task is back on the board. Find deleted tasks with tasks_list(include_deleted: true). The task id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). Deleting detaches a task from its epic and drops its blocker links \u{2014} restoring does not bring those back, set them again with tasks_update. Returns: the restored task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts}";

pub const TASKS_MOVE: &str = "Move a task to another project: project_id is the destination project. The task_id must use the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-82'). Returns: the moved task - {id, project_id, number, title, description, status, priority, is_epic, epic_id, scope_id, source_file, tags, estimate, assignee, due_at, attachments_count, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, closed_reason, blocked_reason, deleted_at, project_name, scope_name, short_description, blocked_by, blocks, relates_to, duplicates, duplicated_by, children_counts}";
